------------------
Let the conditioning input be $x$, and let an encoder produce a context representation:

$$
c = e_\phi(x).
$$

A common initialization is:

$$
h_0 = W_c c + b_c.
$$

The decoder then generates an output sequence autoregressively. Let $z_t$ denote the token fed into the decoder at time $t$. The recurrent update is:

$$
h_t = F_\theta(h_{t-1}, E z_t, c),
$$

where $E z_t$ is the embedding of the decoder input token. In many implementations, the context $c$ is used only to initialize $h_0$; in others, $c$ is also concatenated with the decoder input at every step.

The decoder produces logits:

$$
o_t = W_o h_t + b_o,
$$

and a predictive distribution:

$$
p_\theta(y_t \mid z_{\le t}, x)
=
\operatorname{softmax}(o_t).
$$

For a target output sequence

$$
\hat{y}_{1:T_y}
=
(\hat{y}_1, \hat{y}_2, \ldots, \hat{y}_{T_y}),
$$

the training objective is usually the negative log-likelihood:

$$
\mathcal{L}
=
-\sum_{t=1}^{T_y}
\log p_\theta(\hat{y}_t \mid \hat{y}_{<t}, x).
$$

Equivalently, using cross-entropy at each step:

$$
\mathcal{L}
=
\sum_{t=1}^{T_y}
\operatorname{CE}(p_t, \hat{y}_t),
$$

where

$$
p_t = p_\theta(\cdot \mid \hat{y}_{<t}, x).
$$

During training with **teacher forcing**, the decoder input is the previous ground-truth token:

$$
z_t = \hat{y}_{t-1},
$$

with a special start token

$$
z_1 = \langle \mathrm{BOS} \rangle.
$$

During inference, the previous ground-truth token is unavailable, so the model feeds back its own previous prediction:

$$
z_t = \tilde{y}_{t-1},
$$

where

$$
\tilde{y}_{t-1}
=
\arg\max_y p_\theta(y \mid \tilde{y}_{<t-1}, x)
$$

for greedy decoding, or is sampled/selected by beam search.

Thus the training-time factorization is:

$$
p_\theta(\hat{y}_{1:T_y} \mid x)
=
\prod_{t=1}^{T_y}
p_\theta(\hat{y}_t \mid \hat{y}_{<t}, x),
$$

while inference generates recursively from the model's own previous outputs. This train-test mismatch is known as **exposure bias**, and it is one reason autoregressive generation can accumulate errors.
